= Computer architecture — Technical =

== ISA boundary ==
The ISA defines an abstract machine visible to low-level software:

<pre>
machine state
+ instructions
+ memory/exception semantics
→ architectural behavior
</pre>

Microarchitectures may implement this behavior using very different internal mechanisms.

== Performance ==
A rough decomposition is:

<pre>
execution time
≈ instruction count × cycles/instruction × cycle time
</pre>

but modern systems complicate each factor through caches, speculation, parallelism and heterogeneous units.

== Memory hierarchy ==
Caches exploit temporal and spatial locality.

Latency, bandwidth, associativity, coherence and consistency policies interact with workload behavior.

== Parallel architecture ==
Architectural models include multicore shared memory, SIMD/vector, GPUs, NUMA systems and accelerators.

Performance depends on available parallelism, synchronization, locality and data movement.

